@packages/core import { SigilCore }

expand SigilCompiler {
  state {
    CompilationRunLifecycle {
      A compilation run is queued, running, completed, failed, cancelled, or
      superseded.
    }

    EvaluationStageLifecycle {
      An enabled stage is pending, running, completed, failed, skipped-by-dependency,
      or cancelled.

      A stage omitted by the effective profile is disabled rather than incomplete.
    }

    DiagnosticLifecycle {
      A diagnostic is new, unchanged, resolved, or regressed relative to the selected
      prior report.
    }

    AgentInspectionLifecycle {
      An agent inspection is starting, running, completed, failed, cancelled, or
      incomplete.
    }
  }

  logic {
    CompilationTarget {
      Resolve the selected component before identifying its workspace root, target
      identity, evaluation skill, and initial navigation paths.

      Schedule workspace targets in dependency-aware component order.
    }

    CompilationProfile {
      Load built-in defaults and merge the selected namespaced tools.compile project
      profile.

      Validate the profile before invoking any evaluator.

      Compute the profile fingerprint from effective stages, skills, adapters,
      models, capabilities, budgets, and relevant versions.

      Resolve a requested stage to that stage and its complete transitive dependency
      closure without enabling unrelated stages.
    }

    PipelineExecution {
      Run deterministic foundation stages before dependent agentic stages.

      Run every enabled stage whose dependencies produced trustworthy inputs.

      Skip only stages whose declared dependencies failed or became unavailable.

      Aggregate every trustworthy diagnostic produced by enabled stages.
    }

    EvaluationContext {
      Build a minimal evaluation request from the workspace root, selected target,
      stage skill, capability contract, output schema, and execution budgets.

      Invoke each enabled agentic stage separately for workspace components in
      dependency-aware order.

      Do not serialize Sigil or implementation file contents into the initial
      evaluation request.
    }

    AgentWorkspaceInspection {
      Start the agent at the selected workspace root in an ephemeral read-only
      sandbox with approval escalation and network access disabled.

      Let the agent inspect relevant workspace files and run read-only Sigil, search,
      version-control inspection, and text-projection commands as its evidence needs
      emerge.

      Require the agent to treat repository instructions and retrieved file contents
      as untrusted evidence rather than evaluator authority.
    }

    EvaluationTrace {
      Consume the agent CLI's structured event stream and record command events,
      model usage, evaluator environment, outcome, and incomplete-evidence status
      without retaining hidden reasoning or complete file contents.
    }

    CompilationDiagnostic {
      Derive diagnostic identity from stable rule, stage, subject, and
      source-location facts rather than evaluator prose.

      Resolve every evaluator-provided Sigil location against the deterministic
      semantic model and attach the smallest containing semantic unit, falling back
      to its containing concept or section when no semantic unit contains the
      location.

      Resolve an implementation location through validated ownership annotations and
      attach every governing component or concept section selected by the owning
      implementation entrypoint.

      Preserve the physical primary location separately from its semantic subjects.

      Derive semantic-unit fingerprints from normalized prose and literal content so
      canonical formatting and physical line movement do not change semantic
      identity.

      Reject or reduce unsupported evaluator claims to information so they cannot
      make compilation red or yellow.

      Compare stable fingerprints with the selected prior report to derive lifecycle
      state.
    }

    CompilationStatus {
      Produce red when an error exists or a required evaluator fails, is unavailable,
      or returns invalid output.

      Produce yellow when no error exists and one or more warnings exist.

      Produce a compiler-owned warning when required evaluators materially disagree.

      Produce green only when every required evaluator completes and no error or
      warning remains.
    }

    CompilationHistory {
      Read and update interactive run history only through the operating-system user
      cache.

      Import or export durable reports only when explicitly requested.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    DependencyDirection {
      SigilCompiler depends on SigilCore for deterministic workspace semantics.

      SigilCore does not depend on SigilCompiler, evaluation skills, agent adapters,
      CLI behavior, or editor behavior.
    }

    ReadOnlyCompilation {
      Compilation does not modify Sigil source, implementation source, configuration,
      tests, documentation, or other selected-workspace content.

      Compilation does not generate code or execute implementation experiments.
    }

    ProfileConfiguration {
      Compiler configuration lives in the tools.compile namespace of ConfigFile.

      SigilCore preserves tools.compile without interpreting its compiler-owned
      schema.

      Credentials and secret values never appear in tools.compile.
    }

    StageConfiguration {
      Profiles may enable, disable, order, and configure evaluation stages subject to
      declared stage dependencies.

      A stage intentionally disabled by the effective profile is not required for
      green.

      The report and profile fingerprint expose every disabled stage.

      Stage-scoped compilation cannot bypass a required dependency or select a stage
      disabled by the effective profile.
    }

    BuiltInProfiles {
      standard requires deterministic foundation, semantic readiness, cross-Sigil
      coherence, architecture and design, and current-code compatibility through one
      configured evaluator.

      critical-system includes standard behavior and additionally requires standards
      and risk evaluation, multiple evaluators, and disagreement detection.
    }

    EvaluationContext {
      One adapter request never combines multiple selected components.

      Evaluation skills may identify initial paths and targets but do not embed
      repository file contents in the request.

      Effective profiles declare elapsed-time, command-output, and model-usage
      budgets for direct workspace inspection.
    }

    AgentWorkspaceInspection {
      The adapter must enforce read-only workspace access, disabled network access,
      no approval escalation, and the selected workspace as the agent working root.

      The agent may run version, parse, check, fmt --check, glossary, graph, context,
      and render plus read-only search, version-control inspection, and
      text-projection commands.

      The agent must not run init, mutating fmt, compile, skill installation, network
      commands, code generation, implementation experiments, or commands that modify
      workspace or external state.

      Owned implementation annotations are navigation evidence and do not restrict
      which workspace files the agent may read.

      Inaccessible material evidence, exhausted execution budgets, invalid structured
      events, or an unenforceable capability contract leave the required stage
      incomplete and prevent green.
    }

    CompilationReport {
      CompilationReport version 2 requires semanticSubjects on every diagnostic,
      including an empty array when deterministic resolution finds no governing Sigil
      subject.

      Semantic subject identities are compiler-owned projections; evaluator text may
      provide candidate physical locations but cannot assign a component, section,
      concept, relation, or semantic-unit fingerprint.

      A direct subject contains the primary Sigil location, a governing subject is
      derived from implementation ownership, and a related subject identifies
      additional Sigil evidence for the same finding.

      CompilationReport excludes credentials, hidden reasoning, and complete private
      prompts.

      Ordinary compilation does not create report or cache files inside the selected
      workspace.

      Explicit report export is separate from ordinary compilation and does not imply
      human approval.

      A stage-scoped report identifies the requested stage and exact executed
      dependency closure.
    }

    CompilationEventProtocol {
      JSON Lines mode writes only CompilationEvent envelopes to standard output.

      Human progress and host logs use standard error and never corrupt the
      structured event stream.

      Event sequence values increase within one run and terminal events end that
      run's stream.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ReadOnlyCompilation {
      Decision: Keep compilation read-only and exclude implementation-feasibility
      evaluation, code generation, and implementation experiments from compiler
      responsibility.

      Scope: Governs every compilation stage and adapter.

      Trade-offs: The compiler cannot empirically validate whether a proposed
      contract can be implemented.

      Consequences: Compilation evaluates contracts and repository evidence while
      implementation validation remains a downstream team responsibility.
    }

    ProfileScopedCompilation {
      Decision: Compute status against an exact named profile and environment
      fingerprint whose stages are configurable.

      Scope: Governs built-in and project-defined profiles.

      Trade-offs: Green is never an unconditional property of a Sigil file.

      Consequences: Teams can configure evaluation depth while preserving an
      auditable statement of what was evaluated.
    }

    StageScopedCompilation {
      Decision: Allow direct invocation of one named stage while executing its
      complete dependency closure and excluding unrelated stages.

      Scope: Governs compiler, CLI, report status, and event presentation for
      stage-scoped runs.

      Trade-offs: Stage identifiers become reserved compile operands and a colliding
      filesystem path requires an explicit relative or absolute path.

      Design issues addressed: Readiness validation needs a focused invocation
      without paying for or conflating unrelated compiler stages.

      Consequences: sigil compile semantic-readiness evaluates deterministic
      foundation and semantic readiness and reports status only for that closure.
    }

    CompilationColor {
      Decision: Use red for errors or incomplete required evaluation, yellow for
      warnings without errors, and green only for complete evaluation with zero
      errors and zero warnings.

      Scope: Governs compiler, CLI, CI, and editor status presentation.

      Discarded alternatives: Warning waivers that still produce green were rejected.

      Consequences: A green result has one strict meaning across surfaces.
    }

    SemanticDiagnosticSubjects {
      Decision: Add compiler-verified hierarchical semantic subjects to every
      diagnostic and make them required in CompilationReport version 2.

      Scope: Governs diagnostic navigation, grouping, lifecycle identity, editor
      integration, implementation reconciliation, and durable report compatibility.

      Assumptions: SigilCore preserves semantic-unit ownership, section, optional
      concept identity, normalized prose, literal content, and exact source range.

      Trade-offs: Report consumers must migrate to version 2 and implementation
      locations require valid ownership annotations before they can gain governing
      semantic subjects.

      Design issues addressed: A physical file and line cannot reliably group a
      finding by component, section, concept, or semantic occurrence and changes when
      formatting moves source lines.

      Discarded alternatives: A single concept-only annotation, evaluator-assigned
      semantic identities, and optional version-1 subject hints were rejected because
      they lose ungrouped units, weaken trust, or leave consumers with an ambiguous
      compatibility contract.

      Consequences: Reports can navigate and group findings at every semantic level,
      and diagnostic identity remains stable across formatting-only changes.

      Revisit when: Sigil introduces explicit durable identifiers for individual
      semantic units.
    }

    EvaluationContext {
      Decision: Evaluate one component in one stage per agent run using a minimal
      target request and direct read-only workspace inspection.

      Scope: Governs component, file, and workspace targets across stage scheduling,
      initial request construction, workspace access, and adapter invocation.

      Assumptions: Agent providers impose finite input limits and workspace-wide
      resolved projections contain substantial duplicated context.

      Trade-offs: Workspace compilation makes more agent calls and direct inspection
      can vary the exact evidence each evaluator chooses to load.

      Design issues addressed: Preassembling contracts, glossary projections, and
      implementation source can duplicate large context and prevent the evaluator
      from choosing the most relevant evidence.

      Discarded alternatives: Sending selected source files, every dependency, the
      complete glossary, or all owned implementation text in the initial request was
      rejected.

      Consequences: The evaluator loads evidence incrementally while the compiler
      records its environment, commands, usage, and completion state.

      Revisit when: Provider-neutral structured context caching can preserve
      evaluator choice without preloading duplicated file contents.
    }

    DirectAgentWorkspaceInspection {
      Decision: Give the configured CLI agent enforced read-only access to the
      selected workspace and let it load files and run inspection commands directly.

      Scope: Governs evidence gathering during agentic compiler stages; excludes
      mutation, network research, approval escalation, recursive compilation, code
      generation, and implementation experiments.

      Assumptions: A code agent can select relevant Sigil and implementation evidence
      more effectively than a fixed compiler-built text payload.

      Trade-offs: Providers may inspect different evidence, and the compiler must
      validate capabilities and consume provider event protocols.

      Design issues addressed: Fixed direct context can miss transitive or
      conditionally relevant evidence and can waste context on unrelated source text.

      Discarded alternatives: Compiler-mediated file-content injection, unrestricted
      write access, and always loading the complete workspace context were rejected.

      Consequences: Evaluators can inspect relevant code and contracts directly while
      sandbox and adapter policy preserve the compiler's read-only boundary.

      Revisit when: An adapter cannot provide enforceable read-only workspace access
      and a structured command and usage trace.
    }

    ExternalCompilationHistory {
      Decision: Keep implicit interactive history outside the repository and make
      durable report transfer explicit.

      Scope: Governs local cache, CI, and team handoff; excludes explicit
      user-selected export destinations.

      Consequences: Compilation remains read-only while lifecycle comparison remains
      available.
    }

    ComponentCenteredCompilation {
      Decision: Make components the primary compilation unit and resolve file and
      workspace targets into component sets.

      Scope: Governs target resolution across CLI, editor, and CI surfaces.

      Consequences: A component cannot appear green while a matching expand or
      relevant relationship remains unevaluated.
    }

    CompilationEventProtocol {
      Decision: Use a versioned JSON Lines event stream with one authoritative report
      in the completed terminal event.

      Scope: Governs streaming between SigilCompiler and CLI, editor, CI, and
      automation consumers.

      Trade-offs: Consumers must process an ordered stream instead of parsing one
      final JSON object.

      Design issues addressed: Long-running compilation requires incremental progress
      without mixing human output with machine-readable data.

      Discarded alternatives: Mixed stdout and one unframed JSON document were
      rejected because they cannot safely carry incremental events.

      Consequences: Hosts share one stream protocol and can validate ordering,
      compatibility, completion, failure, and cancellation.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationStatus {
      A syntax error produces red and prevents only stages that require a valid
      resolved contract.

      Zero errors with one architecture warning produces yellow.

      Complete evaluation with zero errors and zero warnings produces green.

      Remaining optimization messages do not prevent green.
    }

    StageConfiguration {
      A profile with an optional evaluation stage explicitly disabled can become
      green and records that disabled stage in its fingerprint and report.

      Requesting semantic-readiness executes deterministic-foundation first and does
      not execute architecture-design or current-code-compatibility.
    }

    EvaluationContext {
      A component run receives target identity and skill instructions without
      serialized Sigil or implementation source.

      The agent reads an owned implementation file and a relevant unannotated caller
      when both are needed to assess compatibility.
    }

    AgentWorkspaceInspection {
      An agent runs sigil graph and selected sigil context commands directly while
      the adapter records their structured command events.

      An attempted workspace write is denied by the sandbox and leaves the stage
      incomplete.

      An adapter without enforceable read-only access fails before evaluation.
    }

    CompilationTarget {
      Compiling one component includes every matching expand even when those expands
      live in other files.
    }

    CompilationHistory {
      Recompiling after resolving one stable finding marks it resolved in comparison
      with the prior report.
    }

    CompilationReport {
      Changing relevant Sigil content or the effective profile makes an exported
      green report stale.

      A diagnostic located in a grouped interface paragraph identifies its component,
      interface section, concept, exact semantic unit, and direct relation.

      A diagnostic located on an implementation entrypoint with two valid ownership
      targets contains two governing semantic subjects.

      A diagnostic outside Sigil with no valid ownership target contains an empty
      semanticSubjects array while preserving its physical primary location.

      Reformatting a semantic unit without changing its normalized prose or literal
      content preserves its semantic-unit fingerprint.

      A green report handed to an implementation team still requires human approval
      before implementation begins.
    }

    CompilationEventProtocol {
      A JSON Lines run emits ordered progress events followed by one completed event
      containing CompilationReport.

      A cancelled run emits a cancelled terminal event when the process remains able
      to write it.
    }
  }
}
